      "title": "Getting around",
      "canDo": "Say when transport leaves and arrives and where to get on or off",
      "tip": {
        "title": "Separable prefixes move to the end",
        "body": "In a main clause, German splits verbs such as **abfahren** and **ankommen**. The conjugated verb takes position 2, while the prefix moves to the end: **Der Zug fährt ab.** English keeps “leave” as one piece.\n\nThe same pattern appears in questions: **Wann kommt der Bus an?** Keep the prefix at the end even when a time word begins the sentence: **Jetzt steigt sie ein.**",
        "examples": [
          { "target": "Der Zug fährt jetzt ab.", "source": "The train is leaving now." },
          { "target": "Wann kommt der Bus an?", "source": "When does the bus arrive?" },
          { "target": "An der Post steige ich aus.", "source": "I get off at the post office." }
        ]
      },
      "words": [
        { "id": "w-abfahren", "target": "abfahren", "source": "to depart", "sourceAccept": ["to leave"], "forms": ["fahre", "fährst", "fährt", "fahrt", "fahren", "ab"], "pos": "verb" },
        { "id": "w-ankommen", "target": "ankommen", "source": "to arrive", "forms": ["komme", "kommst", "kommt", "kommen", "an"], "pos": "verb" },
        { "id": "w-einsteigen", "target": "einsteigen", "source": "to get on", "sourceAccept": ["to board"], "forms": ["steige", "steigst", "steigt", "steigen", "ein"], "pos": "verb" },
        { "id": "w-aussteigen", "target": "aussteigen", "source": "to get off", "sourceAccept": ["to exit"], "forms": ["steige", "steigst", "steigt", "steigen", "aus"], "pos": "verb" },
