    {
      "id": "u19",
      "title": "I can do that",
      "canDo": "Talk about abilities and ask simple questions about permission",
      "tip": {
        "title": "Put the second verb at the end",
        "body": "The modal verb **können** changes to match the subject and takes position 2. The action stays as an infinitive at the end: **Ich kann schwimmen.** English puts “can” before the action too, but German sends the action to the clause end.\n\nIn a question, the modal comes first: **Kann ich hier parken?** For one person, use **kannst du**; for several people, **könnt ihr**; and formally, **können Sie**.",
        "examples": [
          { "target": "Ich kann gut kochen.", "source": "I can cook well." },
          { "target": "Kannst du tanzen?", "source": "Can you dance?" },
          { "target": "Kann ich hier fotografieren?", "source": "Can I take photos here?" }
        ]
      },
